    {
      "id": "eyy8lp3",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "ELECTRICITY"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Electric current (I) is defined as the rate of flow of electric charge (Q = I × t).",
        "Ohm's Law states that V = IR under constant physical conditions such as temperature.",
        "Resistivity (ρ) depends strictly on the material composition and temperature, not on length or area.",
        "In series connections, electric current remains uniform; in parallel connections, voltage remains uniform.",
        "Joule's Law of Heating specifies heat produced as H = I²Rt."
      ],
      "id": "v7kzwq4"
    },
